        "answer": {
          "short": "$xt=120$ and $(x+10)\\left(t+\\frac16\\right)=150$, using distance = speed × time and $10\\text{ min}=\\frac16\\text{ h}$.",
          "worked": "For the car, distance = speed × time gives $xt=120$.\n\nThe train’s speed is $(x+10)$ km/h and its journey time is $\\left(t+\\frac16\\right)$ hours, since $10\\text{ minutes}=\\frac{10}{60}\\text{ hour}=\\frac16\\text{ hour}$. Its distance is 150 km, so\n$$ (x+10)\\left(t+\\frac16\\right)=150. $$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Subtract (1) from (2) to obtain a linear equation linking $x$ and $t$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x+60t=170$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x+10)\\left(t+\\frac16\\right)-xt&=150-120\\\\\\frac{x}{6}+10t+\\frac53&=30\\\\x+60t+10&=180\\\\x+60t&=170\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p35-q3-c-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Make ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "t",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " the subject of this linear equation, substitute it into (1) and solve for ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", obtaining ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x=80\\text{ or }x=90",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t=\\frac{170-x}{60}$; the car’s average speed is $80$ km/h or $90$ km/h.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}60t&=170-x\\\\t&=\\frac{170-x}{60}\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute into (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}x\\left(\\frac{170-x}{60}\\right)&=120\\\\170x-x^2&=7200\\\\x^2-170x+7200&=0\\\\(x-80)(x-90)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=80$ or $x=90$, giving car speeds of 80 km/h or 90 km/h.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 57
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Calculate the corresponding values of $t$ and check that both pairs of solutions make sense.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=80,\\ t=\\frac32$; or $x=90,\\ t=\\frac43$, with speed in km/h and time in hours. Both pairs give the required distances and a train journey 10 minutes longer.",
          "worked": "When $x=80$,\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=\\frac{120}{80}\\\\t&=\\frac32\\text{ h}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe train’s speed is 90 km/h and its time is $\\frac32+\\frac16=\\frac53$ h. The car travels $80\\times\\frac32=120$ km, and the train travels $90\\times\\frac53=150$ km.\n\nWhen $x=90$,\n$$\\begin{align*}t&=\\frac{120}{90}\\\\t&=\\frac43\\text{ h}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe train’s speed is 100 km/h and its time is $\\frac43+\\frac16=\\frac32$ h. The car travels $90\\times\\frac43=120$ km, and the train travels $100\\times\\frac32=150$ km.\n\nBoth pairs have positive speeds and times. In each case the train is 10 km/h faster and takes $\\frac16$ hour, or 10 minutes, longer, so both satisfy the original conditions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 66
      }
    ]
